# Caddy TLS snippet for the PUBLIC server deployment (TLS_MODE=public), e.g. an
# AWS EC2 instance fronted by Cloudflare.
#
# Imported by ../Caddyfile. This tells Caddy to obtain and automatically renew
# a real, publicly-trusted Let's Encrypt certificate for {$SITE_HOST} by solving
# the ACME DNS-01 challenge through the Cloudflare DNS provider module (compiled
# into the custom image built by caddy.Dockerfile).
#
# DNS-01 is used (instead of the default HTTP-01/TLS-ALPN-01) because:
#   * it works when the hostname is proxied through Cloudflare (orange-cloud),
#     which would otherwise interfere with HTTP-01/TLS-ALPN-01, and
#   * it never requires inbound port 80 to be reachable from the internet.
#
# Requirements (see README "Public server deployment"):
#   * SITE_HOST must be a real public FQDN whose zone is managed by Cloudflare.
#   * ACME_EMAIL is the contact address Let's Encrypt uses for expiry/revocation
#     notices (optional, but strongly recommended). If left empty, Caddy issues
#     the certificate without registering a contact email.
#   * CLOUDFLARE_API_TOKEN must be a scoped Cloudflare API token with the
#     permissions Zone.Zone:Read AND Zone.DNS:Edit for the relevant zone. It is
#     read at RUNTIME via the {env.CLOUDFLARE_API_TOKEN} placeholder, so it is
#     never baked into the configuration or cached in the Caddy autosave config.
#
# resolvers 1.1.1.1 forces Caddy to query Cloudflare's own resolver when waiting
# for the _acme-challenge TXT record to propagate, avoiding "timed out waiting
# for record to fully propagate" errors caused by a caching local resolver.
tls {$ACME_EMAIL} {
    dns cloudflare {env.CLOUDFLARE_API_TOKEN}
    resolvers 1.1.1.1
}